target: internal/record/parity_test.go
verify: go test ./internal/record/ -count=1 -run 'TestSaysNoSuchRunReadsBothSignals'
expect: FAIL: TestSaysNoSuchRunReadsBothSignals
why:    comparing the WHOLE stderr buffer against "unknown" is the third wrong version of this guard and the one a reviewer is most likely to restore, because it reads as stricter. It is not stricter, it is brittle: any interpreter warning at import makes a HEALTHY oracle fail the gate, which is the exact sensitivity that separating stderr from stdout was introduced to remove. No fixture puts noise on stderr, so this entry and its test are the only things that can see the regression. Renamed from `reads-one-signal`, which was wrong: this mutation leaves the exit-code half intact, so it does not reduce the guard to one signal.
--- old
	fields := strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(s), "\n")
--- new
	fields := []string{strings.TrimSpace(s)}
